    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations",
    "problem": "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$",
    "worked_solution": "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-2x,\\,{t}^{2}=-4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =4{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-4y$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2} & =-4y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${x}^{2}=-y.$",
